{
  "about": "The one head every app draws bots from (docs/adr/003-one-figure-definition-for-all-apps.md). The botdot server hands it out at GET /api/figure; the web page, Android and the Mac each have a renderer and no face data of their own beyond one built-in bare head. Units are those of 'box'. A bot is 'head' (a circle in the bot's colour), the face of its state and its hat. There are three layers, one inside the other: 'head' (everything), 'eyes' (the face) and 'pupils' (the part of the face that looks around). A state has 'eyes' and 'pupils', each a list of shapes in the box's units (the shapes of docs/adr/004-pieces-as-restricted-shape-lists.md, in the tones main, deep, white and ink; 'tones' gives white and ink), a 'pose' (the still face, shown with reduced motion) and maybe a 'move'. A pose places layers: head { move, turn }, eyes { move, scale }, pupils { move }; move is [x, y], turn is degrees clockwise about pivot.head, scale is [x, y] about pivot.eyes; what a pose leaves out is move [0, 0], turn 0, scale [1, 1]. 'move' loops for ever: each key overrides the pose, with the one easing between each pair of keys. 'attach.hat' puts the origin of the hat's box at 'at' and draws the box 'scale' times as large. 'kit' holds the hats a bot's outfit names: each a list of shapes in the hat's 'box', filled and/or stroked in 'main' (the bot's colour) or 'deep' (the colour mixed 45% towards black on a light background, towards white on a dark one). 'chief' is the Chief of Staff's fixed outfit. 'small' is the mark beside a bot's name in text and for each thing that waits in Needs you: the head with its hat, without eyes, still, with the part 'view' ([x, y, width, height] of the box) filling the mark.",
